   "id": "el-0290",
   "seed": 266782136,
   "versionNonce": 1305241037,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 1234,
   "y": 3440.92,
   "width": 1672,
   "height": 21.96,
   "strokeColor": "#1a1d23",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 18,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "Key modelling decisions (the ones that get argued about in review)",
   "originalText": "Key modelling decisions (the ones that get argued about in review)",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.22,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 14.4
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0291",
   "seed": 739073035,
   "versionNonce": 945628168,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 1234,
   "y": 3470.88,
   "width": 1672,
   "height": 497.0,
   "strokeColor": "#495057",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 12.5,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "1  Hybrid relational + JSONB, deliberately \u2014 not one or the other.\n   The skeleton (category -> product -> variant -> asset -> document, plus the product<->industry m:n)\n   is a fixed graph with real integrity rules, so it is relational with enforced foreign keys. The spec\n   sheet is not fixed: a blast freezer has pull-down time, a wine chiller has bottle count, a serve-over\n   has pane geometry. Those live in product.specs / variant.dims_mm as JSONB behind a GIN index, with a\n   per-category JSON Schema validating them on write. A new product family therefore ships with zero\n   migration, while foreign keys still make orphaned variants and assets impossible.\n\n2  The VARIANT, not the product, is the unit of truth for specs.\n   Williams shows one 'Blast Freezer' page but sells many model codes. Dimensions, capacity, energy class\n   and price belong to the SKU; marketing copy belongs to the product. Collapsing the two is the single\n   mistake that forces a schema rewrite in year two, the moment someone asks for a price list.\n\n3  Bytes never go in the database. asset holds metadata plus a storage_key; S3 holds the file; the CDN\n   serves it. This keeps the Postgres working set in RAM and keeps restores measured in minutes.\n\n4  Content is versioned; demand is immutable. content_revision keeps a full JSONB snapshot per publish.\n   lead and application rows are never edited in place \u2014 status transitions append to audit_log, which is\n   how you answer 'who touched this enquiry, and when' during a commission dispute.\n\n5  Derived stores are disposable. OpenSearch, Redis and ClickHouse can all be dropped and rebuilt from\n   Postgres. Nothing is stored only in them. That rule is what keeps one backup and one restore drill.\n\n6  Indexes come from the actual query shapes, not from habit:\n   GiST on category.path (subtree reads) and on installation.geo (radius search)\n   GIN on product.specs (containment), search_tsv (full text), news_post.tags (array overlap)\n   BRIN on created_at / published_at \u2014 append-only time columns, ~1000x smaller than a B-tree\n   PARTIAL on outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL \u2014 the only genuinely hot scan in the system",
   "originalText": "1  Hybrid relational + JSONB, deliberately \u2014 not one or the other.\n   The skeleton (category -> product -> variant -> asset -> document, plus the product<->industry m:n)\n   is a fixed graph with real integrity rules, so it is relational with enforced foreign keys. The spec\n   sheet is not fixed: a blast freezer has pull-down time, a wine chiller has bottle count, a serve-over\n   has pane geometry. Those live in product.specs / variant.dims_mm as JSONB behind a GIN index, with a\n   per-category JSON Schema validating them on write. A new product family therefore ships with zero\n   migration, while foreign keys still make orphaned variants and assets impossible.\n\n2  The VARIANT, not the product, is the unit of truth for specs.\n   Williams shows one 'Blast Freezer' page but sells many model codes. Dimensions, capacity, energy class\n   and price belong to the SKU; marketing copy belongs to the product. Collapsing the two is the single\n   mistake that forces a schema rewrite in year two, the moment someone asks for a price list.\n\n3  Bytes never go in the database. asset holds metadata plus a storage_key; S3 holds the file; the CDN\n   serves it. This keeps the Postgres working set in RAM and keeps restores measured in minutes.\n\n4  Content is versioned; demand is immutable. content_revision keeps a full JSONB snapshot per publish.\n   lead and application rows are never edited in place \u2014 status transitions append to audit_log, which is\n   how you answer 'who touched this enquiry, and when' during a commission dispute.\n\n5  Derived stores are disposable. OpenSearch, Redis and ClickHouse can all be dropped and rebuilt from\n   Postgres. Nothing is stored only in them. That rule is what keeps one backup and one restore drill.\n\n6  Indexes come from the actual query shapes, not from habit:\n   GiST on category.path (subtree reads) and on installation.geo (radius search)\n   GIN on product.specs (containment), search_tsv (full text), news_post.tags (array overlap)\n   BRIN on created_at / published_at \u2014 append-only time columns, ~1000x smaller than a B-tree\n   PARTIAL on outbox WHERE published_at IS NULL \u2014 the only genuinely hot scan in the system",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.42,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 10.0
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0292",
   "seed": 995199739,
   "versionNonce": 2131947575,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 4051.88,
   "width": 2920,
   "height": 28.799999999999997,
   "strokeColor": "#1a1d23",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 24,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "4 \u00b7 Why PostgreSQL \u2014 and explicitly why not the alternatives",
   "originalText": "4 \u00b7 Why PostgreSQL \u2014 and explicitly why not the alternatives",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.2,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 19.200000000000003
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0293",
   "seed": 575068674,
   "versionNonce": 158160249,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 4086.6800000000003,
   "width": 2920,
   "height": 19.599999999999998,
   "strokeColor": "#6c757d",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 14,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "A database choice is only defensible next to the options it beat. Decision record: ADR-0001, revisit trigger = p95 primary CPU > 60% for 7 days.",
   "originalText": "A database choice is only defensible next to the options it beat. Decision record: ADR-0001, revisit trigger = p95 primary CPU > 60% for 7 days.",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.4,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 11.200000000000001
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0294",
   "seed": 1831255262,
   "versionNonce": 323382729,
   "type": "rectangle",
   "x": 0,
   "y": 4126.280000000001,
   "width": 1180,
   "height": 646.15,
   "strokeColor": "#2b8a3e",
   "backgroundColor": "#d3f9d8",
   "roundness": {
    "type": 3
   }
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0295",
   "seed": 133010871,
   "versionNonce": 389617316,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 14,
   "y": 4140.280000000001,
   "width": 1152,
   "height": 24.4,
   "strokeColor": "#1a1d23",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 20,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "PostgreSQL \u2014 chosen",
   "originalText": "PostgreSQL \u2014 chosen",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.22,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 16.0
  },
  {
   "isDeleted": false,
   "fillStyle": "solid",
   "strokeWidth": 2,
   "strokeStyle": "solid",
   "roughness": 0,
   "opacity": 100,
   "angle": 0,
   "groupIds": [],
   "frameId": null,
   "boundElements": null,
   "updated": 1,
   "link": null,
   "locked": false,
   "version": 1,
   "id": "el-0296",
   "seed": 901550429,
   "versionNonce": 1999939490,
   "type": "text",
   "x": 14,
   "y": 4172.68,
   "width": 1152,
   "height": 585.75,
   "strokeColor": "#495057",
   "backgroundColor": "transparent",
   "fontSize": 12.5,
   "fontFamily": 2,
   "text": "The shape of the domain fits it.\n   Roughly 90% of this system is relational with hard integrity rules; the remaining 10% (spec sheets,\n   page blocks, UTM payloads, ATS answers) is document-shaped. Postgres is the only mainstream engine\n   that is genuinely first class at both. JSONB + GIN gives you MongoDB's flexibility without giving up\n   joins, foreign keys or transactions \u2014 so the flexibility argument for Mongo simply disappears.\n\nThe one thing that must not break is transactional.\n   A dropped enquiry is lost revenue. lead + lead_item + outbox must commit or fail as a unit. ACID gives\n   that in a single statement, with no distributed-transaction machinery and no dual-write race.\n\nIt absorbs the next three roadmap features without introducing a new datastore:\n   full-text search      -> tsvector + pg_trgm, good to ~10^5 docs, so OpenSearch is deferred not required\n   dealer / install map  -> PostGIS radius and nearest-neighbour queries\n   'similar products', AI -> pgvector embeddings alongside the row they describe\n   cabinet IoT telemetry -> TimescaleDB hypertables in the same instance\n   One engine to operate, back up, monitor, tune and hire for. That is the real cost saving.\n\nRead scaling here is boring and proven.\n   Streaming read replicas + PgBouncer absorb a 100x read spike, and 95% of reads never reach the\n   database because the CDN already answered. Writes are in the hundreds per day \u2014 a single primary is\n   the correct answer for years, and saying so is part of the design, not an omission.\n\nChange data capture is native.\n   Logical decoding + Debezium turns every commit into an event for search, cache purge and the\n   warehouse. Combined with the outbox table this removes the dual-write bug class entirely.\n\nIt is cheap, portable, and hard to get locked into.\n   RDS, Aurora, Cloud SQL, Neon, Supabase or a plain VM \u2014 same SQL, same dump. The exit cost is a\n   pg_dump, which is exactly the property you want in a vendor decision you cannot yet justify revisiting.\n\nLimits we accept, with the mitigation written down:\n   single writer (fine at this write volume) \u00b7 vacuum and bloat need monitoring \u00b7 connection cost needs\n   PgBouncer \u00b7 major-version upgrades need a rehearsed blue/green drill. All standard, all in the runbook.",
   "originalText": "The shape of the domain fits it.\n   Roughly 90% of this system is relational with hard integrity rules; the remaining 10% (spec sheets,\n   page blocks, UTM payloads, ATS answers) is document-shaped. Postgres is the only mainstream engine\n   that is genuinely first class at both. JSONB + GIN gives you MongoDB's flexibility without giving up\n   joins, foreign keys or transactions \u2014 so the flexibility argument for Mongo simply disappears.\n\nThe one thing that must not break is transactional.\n   A dropped enquiry is lost revenue. lead + lead_item + outbox must commit or fail as a unit. ACID gives\n   that in a single statement, with no distributed-transaction machinery and no dual-write race.\n\nIt absorbs the next three roadmap features without introducing a new datastore:\n   full-text search      -> tsvector + pg_trgm, good to ~10^5 docs, so OpenSearch is deferred not required\n   dealer / install map  -> PostGIS radius and nearest-neighbour queries\n   'similar products', AI -> pgvector embeddings alongside the row they describe\n   cabinet IoT telemetry -> TimescaleDB hypertables in the same instance\n   One engine to operate, back up, monitor, tune and hire for. That is the real cost saving.\n\nRead scaling here is boring and proven.\n   Streaming read replicas + PgBouncer absorb a 100x read spike, and 95% of reads never reach the\n   database because the CDN already answered. Writes are in the hundreds per day \u2014 a single primary is\n   the correct answer for years, and saying so is part of the design, not an omission.\n\nChange data capture is native.\n   Logical decoding + Debezium turns every commit into an event for search, cache purge and the\n   warehouse. Combined with the outbox table this removes the dual-write bug class entirely.\n\nIt is cheap, portable, and hard to get locked into.\n   RDS, Aurora, Cloud SQL, Neon, Supabase or a plain VM \u2014 same SQL, same dump. The exit cost is a\n   pg_dump, which is exactly the property you want in a vendor decision you cannot yet justify revisiting.\n\nLimits we accept, with the mitigation written down:\n   single writer (fine at this write volume) \u00b7 vacuum and bloat need monitoring \u00b7 connection cost needs\n   PgBouncer \u00b7 major-version upgrades need a rehearsed blue/green drill. All standard, all in the runbook.",
   "textAlign": "left",
   "verticalAlign": "top",
   "containerId": null,
   "lineHeight": 1.42,
   "autoResize": false,
   "baseline": 10.0
  },
